   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 122 of the laws of 2015 relating to tax assessments\nfor certain improved properties affected by Superstorm Sandy, in relation to\nextending the deadline for tax exemption applications",
    "basePrintNo": "S7339",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7339-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 8,
       "fullName": "John E. Brooks",
       "imgName": "923_John_Brooks.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1101,
       "sessionMemberId": 1097,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "BROOKS"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1 & 2, Chap 122 of 2015",
    "lawSection": "Taxation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7339\n\nSPONSOR: BOYLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 122 of the laws of 2015 relat-\ning to tax assessments for certain improved properties affected by\nSuperstorm Sandy, in relation to extending the deadline for tax\nexemption applications\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nExtends a tax exemption for owner occupied residential property in muni-\ncipalities affected by Superstorm Sandy.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 (d) specifies under what circumstances an exemption shall be\ngranted. Excludes additions and improvements which did not exist before\nSuperstorm Sandy. Section 1 (e) specifies how the exemption shall be\nfiled and extends the duration of the exemption. Section 2 sets out the\neffective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nExtends the deadline for certain Sandy tax exemptions from March 1, 2018\nto March 1, 2020.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs we mark the fifth anniversary of Superstorm Sandy, many storm-impact-\ned homeowners continue to do extensive repairs and reconstruction so\nthat they can remain in or return to their homes.  As a result for many\nhomeowners, their property assessments have increased substantially and\nthey still face the financial burdens of loan payments for the recon-\nstruction in addition to increased property taxes. This legislation\nextends by two years the exemption which provides a graduated tax\nincrease over an eight year period for certain eligible Superstorm Sandy\nvictims.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2015-A7274\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall apply to exemption\napplications made prior to the first of March next succeeding the date\non which this act shall have become a law and no later than the first of\nMarch two thousand twenty, and any exemption once granted shall continue\nfor a period of up to eight prospective tax years as set forth in this\nact.\n",
